so I'm I'm Bob DeSimone I'd like to  welcome you to the annual McGovern  Institute symposium this year's  symposium that has the had its topic  selected by mayor de Jesse re who also  put together the the whole program and I  know he has a goal for the symposium and  by the end we will all have an internal  model of what an internal model is my  role is only to thank all the speakers  for coming thank the audience for for  being here and to thank the McGovern  staff who organized the whole event this  is an all-hands-on-deck kind of event  and I know they worked very hard to make  this a smooth-running a bet so I will  turn it over mayor dad I'm really really  pleased with a sort of symposium lineup  of speakers we have probably most of you  know most of the speakers the initial  point of this symposium was some time  ago when Bob suggested that maybe I  should think about the next annual  symposium for McGovern and what what  would be a topic that I'll choose if I  were to to organize one I thought for a  while and I think one of them sort of  one of the more elegant conceptual  frameworks that has entered the field of  neuroscience in the past several decades  are ideas that come sort of in an  interdisciplinary form bring together  concepts from engineering from sort of  theoretical modeling and experimental  work and and one of the more promising  ones among the various possibilities  that are out there is the concept of  internal models partly because it's  ambiguous but but hopefully the point of  this symposium would be to actually  think a little bit harder about this  ambiguity and whether we can bring  together some some sort of a common  understanding that will bear on models  and mechanisms across scales  and you know I want to say a little bit  about the speakers will will mostly talk  about the ideas around internal models  although I I have my my thoughts that  actually I'm even among the speakers  some will have you know views against  very strong sort of claims about what  internal models are and what they're  used for it's it's not an easy concept  to embrace specially if you want to you  know go go a little bit deeper about  what what you mean but the idea is that  the big why it's valuable it's fairly  straightforward you know a lot of things  that we do in all sorts of behaviors  that we engage in involve you know  several main conceptual problems that  thinking about intermodules comes to  help one is ambiguity you know the same  sensory input can have multiple valid  interpretations you know all of this  famous I don't even know where the  source of it comes from this but this  class this this dress illusion blue blue  black versus gold you know like this  that's the same input we all see and we  have very different interpretations of  it across the the population and perhaps  the reason is internally we think about  sources of light and all sorts of other  things that must must have been in  certain ways so that the interpretation  would make sense for one brain in  certain way for another in another you  know in a different way and it goes  across scales it's not just for visual  illusions of that kind  you know from as Nate's talk will will  indicate you know the simple act of  whether knowing a sensory input is  coming from the outside or from my  movements of my own body is a  fundamental ambiguous problem that the  brain has to solve and that requires  some understanding internally of what it  means if I move my own hands in other  words the sensory areas might have some  sort of a model of the consequence of  the motor command  similarly goes for another concept like  ambiguity which is uncertainty it is  fundamental that you know all the  internal activities inside our brains  and also from  the outside the information has has  various forms of uncertainty associated  with it and is fundamental to the way we  behave that we make inferences about the  state of the world based on not perfect  information and the way to do that again  is to have some knowledge about the  statistical structure of the world world  might be a particular brain area that  another brain area wants to make use of  so if area B wants to listen to area a  and if area a has some variability  associated with it well the only way we  can make sense of it if it has some  understanding of the language that the  the area a speaks and it also goes  fundamentally in a sort of into the  notion of dynamics as soon as you think  about a dynamical system and we behave  in a heavily dynamical environment then  we are dealing with situations where the  smallest forms of noise and variability  will propagate into a dynamical system  into generate more and more erroneous  kind of a consequences and States and  therefore as a as a good engineer always  you will need some sort of a feedback or  control mechanism to to harness the  dynamics and as soon as you want to do  that then the control mechanism needs to  have an understanding of the way the  system can go awry or what are the  sources of noise in the system so all of  these concepts bring together the need  for some part of the brain to have some  knowledge about how another part of the  brain operates and that's in the  broadest sense the the idea behind an  internal model now the language that we  often apply to think about internal  models at least from a sort of a  computational perspective  just because ambiguity uncertainty and  noise and dynamics all are well captured  by the language of probabilities it's an  easy formulation to bring into sort of  our world of understanding to learn  something about these notions of models  but there is a there's a tension there  you know just as a an apple falling from  the sky we might have an equation that  explains  it's dynamics it doesn't mean that the  Apple actually implements that equation  and the same way as an experimenter when  we come up with an equation to explain a  particular behavior that doesn't mean  that the brain actually implements that  exact same equation and that's the  tension between having models to explain  a behavior versus what is it that the  neural circuits actually implement and  that's it's really high at the bridge  between algorithmic modeling concepts  and neural circuit motifs and what I  hope to invite all of you to think about  as the speakers go through their  presentations is whether whether the the  the knowledge so far will lend itself to  building a bridge of that kind where we  can link computational models that  explain behavior at a cognitive  psychophysical level to more lower level  neural circuit mechanisms that in  perhaps smaller animal models have been  studied and if not other ways that we  can adjust either the way we formulate  the models or the way we interrogate the  neural circuits which can bring about a  common language between the two because  it's a fundamental sort of a disconnect  that still exists between models of  cognition and circuits of sensory motor  system in lower animal models and with  with that in mind I just want to tell  you the the sort of the plan for the day  is that we will have half of the  speakers 5 of the speakers will speak  during the morning there will be a break  I I think you all have the program there  will be a break in the middle but the  the morning talks are more geared  towards neural circuit lower-level  mechanisms and then after lunch there  will be another 5 talks with another  break and the talks in the afternoon are  sort of slightly more bent towards  system level descriptions computational  principles and cognitive level sort of  representations of internal models again  with all in  it's that and at the end of the day  we'll have a panel discussion with you  know some of the experts on both ends of  the aisle hopefully engaging each other  in in in ways that you know will will  either we'll learn there is really no  connection between what a sensorimotor  lower level understanding of an internal  model and what we call a cognitive  internal model or maybe there are  linkages and there are circuit motifs  that we can think of that could even be  used to understand more high-level  aspects of internal models and without  without further ado I'll pass on the  podium to our first chair mark Arnett's
 

